1
00:00:15,022 --> 00:00:21,946
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I know we've spoken about meditation techniques and methods before.

2
00:00:22,546 --> 00:00:33,112
[SPEAKER_01]: We've covered that many times, but just as a... to cover it again, maybe with some... to become clearer to others.

3
00:00:37,371 --> 00:00:48,408
[SPEAKER_01]: What is... What do you think about common meditation methods or techniques such, you know, as...

4
00:00:49,230 --> 00:00:52,294
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't really want to mention other people's names or techniques, but...

5
00:00:53,328 --> 00:00:58,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in general, like contemporary meditation techniques...

6
00:00:58,070 --> 00:01:00,290
[SPEAKER_01]: When you google Buddhism, it's meditation.

7
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:01,691
[SPEAKER_01]: The first thing that comes up.

8
00:01:02,711 --> 00:01:08,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the point that we've been making in all the previous talks on meditation, really.

9
00:01:09,013 --> 00:01:20,497
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the... you cannot possibly... you can't possibly do those practices without the implicit view behind it.

10
00:01:21,772 --> 00:01:34,443
[SPEAKER_00]: View that certain performance, observance, mechanical concentration surrendered to the perception, will somehow magically transcend you into the sphere of knowledge.

11
00:01:35,264 --> 00:01:36,185
[SPEAKER_00]: That is implicit.

12
00:01:37,386 --> 00:01:47,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, some people might really have their view really developed, some people might not have it so firmly developed, but if you think that meditation technique

13
00:01:48,508 --> 00:01:55,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Can somehow result in some knowledge through itself, means you're partaking in that view.

14
00:01:55,834 --> 00:01:57,355
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's completely wrong.

15
00:01:59,557 --> 00:02:09,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, when it... meditation technique is not so much... well, it is obviously about technique you do, but it's really... what's even more important is the view you have behind it.

16
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:18,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when we talk about meditation is not a method, in other words, regarding things as a method is your view.

17
00:02:19,616 --> 00:02:28,142
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, like, once I remember in the past, sometimes I explain this to people and then it was like, ah, okay, so, your method is the no-method method.

18
00:02:28,783 --> 00:02:30,964
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, that's not what I'm saying.

19
00:02:31,625 --> 00:02:40,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they will think that denial of the methods basically can... well, they don't think, they take denial or refusal of the methods, they take it as another method.

20
00:02:41,511 --> 00:02:42,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

21
00:02:42,312 --> 00:02:44,974
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of how you take things that you do.

22
00:02:45,955 --> 00:02:47,036
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the implicit view?

23
00:02:47,096 --> 00:02:49,599
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you believe those things can result in?

24
00:02:50,239 --> 00:02:51,240
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the problem is.

25
00:02:51,961 --> 00:02:56,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And that problem, sure, is made worse when you blindly just follow a technique.

26
00:02:58,567 --> 00:03:03,312
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, it already implies that view and now you're just doubling down on it.

27
00:03:04,613 --> 00:03:14,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Through really no thinking for yourself, but the opposite, trying to avoid all the thinking and just concentrate and concentrate through watching your nostrils or, I don't know, whatever else contemporary meditation might be making you do.

28
00:03:17,203 --> 00:03:19,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the view behind you want to see that.

29
00:03:19,664 --> 00:03:30,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I holding a view that if I sit and concentrate on the nostrils, I will have an experience of a special kind that will somehow result in knowledge or be the knowledge?

30
00:03:32,294 --> 00:03:33,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already wrong view.

31
00:03:35,112 --> 00:03:37,553
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already basically a fetter of Sīla Bhārata Paramāsa.

32
00:03:37,994 --> 00:03:43,857
[SPEAKER_00]: My virtue, my duty, if I adhere to it, will result in wisdom and liberation.

33
00:03:46,058 --> 00:03:50,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the first fetter, because of which you will not get wisdom and liberation.

34
00:03:52,841 --> 00:03:56,063
[SPEAKER_00]: So, usually methods and practices are within that fetter.

35
00:03:56,503 --> 00:04:01,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why people would often be quite touchy about it as well, if somebody criticizes their meditation method.

36
00:04:02,466 --> 00:04:03,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if criticism is wrong,

37
00:04:04,472 --> 00:04:06,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Say somebody criticizes unjustly some method.

38
00:04:08,274 --> 00:04:10,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you emotionally feeling defensive?

39
00:04:12,576 --> 00:04:14,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there is no dispassion there.

40
00:04:23,062 --> 00:04:28,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's how you take things, what you... how you regard them, how you... what you assume behind them.

41
00:04:29,665 --> 00:04:30,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So the point is then,

42
00:04:32,527 --> 00:04:34,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Not so much about what you do.

43
00:04:34,589 --> 00:04:38,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what you do is you keep the precepts, of course.

44
00:04:38,171 --> 00:04:41,474
[SPEAKER_00]: You restrain your senses, your eye, your nose, and so on.

45
00:04:42,655 --> 00:04:45,057
[SPEAKER_00]: You're devoted to wakefulness, as the Buddha would say.

46
00:04:45,977 --> 00:04:47,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, try to not lapse, basically.

47
00:04:47,358 --> 00:04:49,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to not lose the sight of the context of your situation.

48
00:04:50,641 --> 00:04:52,042
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a form of samādhi.

49
00:04:54,894 --> 00:04:59,457
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, if you really want to develop the samādhi that pertains to liberation, well, you need the right view.

50
00:04:59,577 --> 00:05:02,438
[SPEAKER_00]: As Samādhita Sutta says, there is no samādhi without the right view.

51
00:05:03,599 --> 00:05:10,063
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't mean you shouldn't try, but what you want to understand is that it's not about, I'll try this technique or try that technique.

52
00:05:10,163 --> 00:05:23,771
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about, I must try to basically uproot my assumption of technique resulting in knowledge, and instead see a direct way to knowledge, whereby I will know how to develop it and pursue it.

53
00:05:25,047 --> 00:05:30,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it's always like he knows that he knows, for a Sotāpanna, for people who develop understanding.

54
00:05:31,269 --> 00:05:39,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, I watched my nostrils, then I had this super calming experience, it was just out of this world, and that's my knowledge.

55
00:05:40,652 --> 00:05:42,353
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's just a novelty experience.

56
00:05:46,794 --> 00:05:48,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge is that which you understand.

57
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:50,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you know how it comes about?

58
00:05:50,631 --> 00:05:51,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we say.

59
00:05:51,412 --> 00:05:52,352
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the origin.

60
00:05:53,013 --> 00:05:55,335
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the... the... the danger.

61
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:56,335
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the escape.

62
00:05:56,416 --> 00:05:57,396
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the cessation.

63
00:05:57,496 --> 00:05:58,057
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things.

64
00:05:58,617 --> 00:05:59,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you fully understand it.

65
00:06:00,018 --> 00:06:05,362
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no chance there, or magical belief that, oh, maybe, hopefully, it will happen like this, or happen like that.

66
00:06:05,723 --> 00:06:07,844
[SPEAKER_00]: When I concentrate on this, hopefully, this will arise.

67
00:06:07,864 --> 00:06:08,125
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

68
00:06:08,505 --> 00:06:09,666
[SPEAKER_00]: You know exactly.

69
00:06:09,726 --> 00:06:12,608
[SPEAKER_00]: If you attend to these things, this is where the mind is heading.

70
00:06:12,968 --> 00:06:15,650
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't attend to these things, this is where the mind is heading.

71
00:06:16,670 --> 00:06:21,112
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the clarity of awareness and mindfulness is basically what samādhi is.

72
00:06:22,453 --> 00:06:24,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Composing that same mindfulness that you have.

73
00:06:26,995 --> 00:06:34,619
[SPEAKER_00]: But that would be impossible for you to do for as long as you don't abandon the view of a method that I just described.

74
00:06:35,379 --> 00:06:35,980
[SPEAKER_00]: If you still

75
00:06:37,025 --> 00:06:46,331
[SPEAKER_00]: On a level of a view, assume or hold an assumption that if you go through the right motions or attend to right things, the knowledge will happen to you.

76
00:06:46,972 --> 00:06:48,233
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it won't happen to you.

77
00:06:50,354 --> 00:06:55,137
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way for knowledge to happen to you is to understand what knowledge is and then to develop it through your own effort.

78
00:06:55,738 --> 00:07:00,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's say that a person is doing... he comes across mindfulness of breathing.

79
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:26,577
[SPEAKER_01]: But he doesn't have that view that if I do this breathing step-by-step technique, breathing in 10 seconds, breathing out 10 seconds, whatever the technique might be, he doesn't have the view that it will lead to magical insight, or just out of the blue it's going to be liberated.

80
00:07:27,410 --> 00:07:30,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's... yeah, he doesn't need to have such a coarse view.

81
00:07:30,233 --> 00:07:30,854
[SPEAKER_00]: That's...

82
00:07:30,874 --> 00:07:31,255
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't...

83
00:07:31,715 --> 00:07:33,097
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't alluding at that.

84
00:07:34,599 --> 00:07:42,788
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is not to know where the place of mindfulness of breathing is, what it does, and you won't be doing it rightly.

85
00:07:42,969 --> 00:07:45,632
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why ānāpānasati is taught to those with the right view.

86
00:07:48,538 --> 00:07:50,679
[SPEAKER_00]: To get rid of the remaining asuras and kilesas.

87
00:07:51,159 --> 00:07:55,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't have the right view, you have no ānāpānasati, no basis for ānāpānasati.

88
00:07:56,061 --> 00:08:02,764
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but then I consider, well, I don't have the right view, but here's this ānāpānasati, so let me try do it.

89
00:08:03,665 --> 00:08:03,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

90
00:08:04,445 --> 00:08:08,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would be impossible for you to do it without the implicit view of a method behind.

91
00:08:09,247 --> 00:08:10,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you will not be able to do it rightly.

92
00:08:12,078 --> 00:08:21,263
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say it will be impossible for you to do it without implicit view of the method, in other words, I'm saying it's impossible for you to do it without your sīlabhāta-paramāsa.

93
00:08:22,503 --> 00:08:28,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to even try ānāpānasati rightly, get the right view, as in abandoned sīlabhāta-paramāsa.

94
00:08:28,647 --> 00:08:31,548
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that is the first factor that's abandoned.

95
00:08:31,608 --> 00:08:33,990
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the first three factors that is abandoned for Sotāpanna.

96
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:38,372
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, yes, there are no methods for him, because he sees directly.

97
00:08:39,568 --> 00:08:41,648
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what path to nibbāna is.

98
00:08:41,668 --> 00:08:48,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in a mystical manner, not in some magical transcendental kind of outcome of certain practices.

99
00:08:48,410 --> 00:08:49,730
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he understands.

100
00:08:49,750 --> 00:08:54,871
[SPEAKER_00]: If I abstain from this and cultivate this, my mind will be liberated, as clear as a day.

101
00:08:54,891 --> 00:09:03,733
[SPEAKER_00]: He might be lazy and negligent and not choose to abstain from things sufficiently enough, so he might not become an arahant in this life, but nevertheless, he knows.

102
00:09:04,493 --> 00:09:05,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And he knows that he knows.

103
00:09:09,012 --> 00:09:15,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all the previous talks we did about the method and techniques, just bundle all that into Sīla Bhātta Paramāsa, because that's what it is, practically speaking.

104
00:09:18,031 --> 00:09:23,213
[SPEAKER_01]: But that method, that technique, that fetter, calms me down.

105
00:09:24,073 --> 00:09:26,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not, you know, doing anything bad.

106
00:09:26,554 --> 00:09:29,175
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not drinking, keeping the precepts, whatever.

107
00:09:29,335 --> 00:09:30,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

108
00:09:30,236 --> 00:09:30,996
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm a good person.

109
00:09:31,356 --> 00:09:31,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

110
00:09:31,576 --> 00:09:32,376
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it calms me down.

111
00:09:32,677 --> 00:09:34,577
[SPEAKER_01]: That method, which is the fetter.

112
00:09:34,597 --> 00:09:34,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, great.

113
00:09:35,037 --> 00:09:38,499
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to be a good bhutujjina, yeah, you can do methods, no problem.

114
00:09:39,599 --> 00:09:40,619
[SPEAKER_00]: But we're not talking about that.

115
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:41,940
[SPEAKER_00]: We're talking about arahantship.

116
00:09:43,588 --> 00:09:57,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to become a good person that doesn't drink and go out and comes through concentrating on your nostrils, or you can do any concentration and calming and breathing technique and psychotherapy or whatever, it's going to make you a better worldly person.

117
00:09:58,073 --> 00:09:59,333
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to free you from the world.

118
00:10:01,274 --> 00:10:04,975
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't conflate the two and then you can decide for yourself what you want to do.

119
00:10:06,353 --> 00:10:07,314
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what I'm saying.

120
00:10:07,694 --> 00:10:09,916
[SPEAKER_00]: The worst is when you think you can have it both ways.

121
00:10:10,596 --> 00:10:11,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I know I don't know.

122
00:10:11,938 --> 00:10:28,191
[SPEAKER_00]: I know I don't have a reference for what truly calm mind is, what truly liberated mind is, but this suits me, this meditation suits me, and I will do it, and I will also kind of implicitly believe that it is bringing me closer to nibbāna, although I have no reference point whatsoever.

123
00:10:28,691 --> 00:10:31,133
[SPEAKER_00]: That's nothing other than wishful thinking.

124
00:10:31,193 --> 00:10:35,817
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you can do it if you want, but it's actually, if you realize for yourself, actually that's quite a perilous thing,

125
00:10:38,995 --> 00:10:42,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about doing a technique?

126
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:46,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Now that you've said that to me...

127
00:10:46,682 --> 00:10:49,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll still do my technique, but I know that I'm not free from suffering.

128
00:10:50,165 --> 00:10:55,169
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll keep doing my calm technique, calming technique of breathing.

129
00:10:56,810 --> 00:10:58,171
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that I'm not free from suffering.

130
00:10:58,191 --> 00:10:59,652
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that I don't have right view.

131
00:11:00,093 --> 00:11:01,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll keep an open mind.

132
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:04,140
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, yeah, you have to start somewhere, I suppose.

133
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:07,281
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, your start there is not with the technique you're doing.

134
00:11:07,741 --> 00:11:11,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Your start there is with what you just heard I'm telling you.

135
00:11:12,402 --> 00:11:23,184
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, even if you had no technique, whatever you were doing on account of practice was your technique, so to speak, even if it wasn't very formally defined.

136
00:11:23,244 --> 00:11:26,784
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to do anything to be within the fetter of virtue and duty.

137
00:11:27,864 --> 00:11:31,885
[SPEAKER_00]: The duty of observance, the duty of concentration, the duty of my sīla and so on.

138
00:11:32,742 --> 00:11:33,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You are within it.

139
00:11:35,986 --> 00:11:40,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about finding the least harmful technique or something like that.

140
00:11:40,951 --> 00:11:44,835
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's about actually hearing the instruction.

141
00:11:44,975 --> 00:11:45,936
[SPEAKER_00]: They will make you think.

142
00:11:46,813 --> 00:11:48,854
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically in the context above your technique.

143
00:11:48,934 --> 00:11:52,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so whatever I'm doing is a technique, but how am I regarding it?

144
00:11:53,137 --> 00:11:54,398
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my view behind it?

145
00:11:55,039 --> 00:12:07,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you choose now, after you heard me, what I just said to you, if you choose to adhere to your technique, well, okay, maybe you still don't have faith in what I said, so, you know, you're doubtful, but at least use that.

146
00:12:07,607 --> 00:12:13,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so let me then think, what are my hopes, assumptions, implications behind the technique I'm doing?

147
00:12:14,132 --> 00:12:15,653
[SPEAKER_00]: What are my thoughts in regard to this?

148
00:12:16,373 --> 00:12:17,634
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I hoping to achieve?

149
00:12:19,995 --> 00:12:23,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I even know what my goal is of my own meditation?

150
00:12:23,896 --> 00:12:29,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I just sort of decided, you know, as the day goes by, and depending on how I feel that time.

151
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:32,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I know the outcome?

152
00:12:33,621 --> 00:12:36,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have the true measure of wholesome calmness?

153
00:12:37,963 --> 00:12:43,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I just calm in the same sense you're calm after you wake up or after a full night of sleep?

154
00:12:45,119 --> 00:12:51,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I calm because my mind cannot be perturbed by suffering and anything that comes through the senses, because I'm in control of my senses?

155
00:12:52,101 --> 00:12:53,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Which calmness is it?

156
00:12:53,941 --> 00:13:05,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I calm because I've basically surrounded myself with a soothing environment that never challenges me, never disturbs my senses, there is no heat, cold, insects, other people, voices, sounds, noises?

157
00:13:06,285 --> 00:13:10,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I calm because all of these things, when they arise, I am unmoved by those things?

158
00:13:14,768 --> 00:13:16,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I precious about my meditation time?

159
00:13:17,991 --> 00:13:23,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I must have my safe space, where nobody will disturb me, so I can sit and concentrate, because there is no other way I can concentrate?

160
00:13:24,556 --> 00:13:34,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I can concentrate at any... in any given scenario, even if I end up in a hospital, immobile, in a cast, I can't move, surrounded by 50 other patients.

161
00:13:34,165 --> 00:13:36,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's basically where your life is heading.

162
00:13:38,424 --> 00:13:39,805
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's say... okay, so...

163
00:14:06,865 --> 00:14:15,750
[SPEAKER_01]: I was doing a meditation technique, and I've heard what you say, and I abandoned that technique of breathing.

164
00:14:15,770 --> 00:14:16,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so what do you do now?

165
00:14:17,531 --> 00:14:17,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

166
00:14:18,751 --> 00:14:20,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So now I'm trying to find out what is right for you.

167
00:14:21,212 --> 00:14:21,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

168
00:14:21,713 --> 00:14:23,654
[SPEAKER_01]: So I need to find out what is right for you.

169
00:14:24,434 --> 00:14:25,034
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I get it?

170
00:14:25,054 --> 00:14:25,515
[SPEAKER_01]: What is this?

171
00:14:26,835 --> 00:14:27,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Where do I start?

172
00:14:30,653 --> 00:14:42,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you start by listening to more of the instructions they direct you in their way, by rereading the suttas that you read before and new suttas that you haven't read, with this new, basically fresh set of eyes, so to speak.

173
00:14:42,723 --> 00:14:46,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, that's what you're looking for now, where the right view is.

174
00:14:50,110 --> 00:14:51,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yeah, what else you do?

175
00:14:51,371 --> 00:14:52,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddha says what you do.

176
00:14:53,533 --> 00:14:54,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Restrained,

177
00:14:55,026 --> 00:14:56,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue is seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

178
00:14:57,588 --> 00:14:59,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you seeing the danger in the slightest fault?

179
00:14:59,309 --> 00:15:03,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that the context in your mind that you invest effort to maintain throughout the day?

180
00:15:05,133 --> 00:15:05,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it?

181
00:15:05,913 --> 00:15:08,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, by the way, there is no meditation without sīla.

182
00:15:09,756 --> 00:15:14,919
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you disregard everything I say about techniques, if you get offended or whatever else, doesn't matter.

183
00:15:15,760 --> 00:15:20,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself, whatever technique you're doing, is your sīla impeccable underneath it?

184
00:15:22,245 --> 00:15:28,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is your sīla good enough, by your own measure, so that you can go and develop your perfect technique?

185
00:15:29,233 --> 00:15:32,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no meditation without full restraint of the senses.

186
00:15:33,758 --> 00:15:37,703
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you spend your day restrained, celibate, withdrawn?

187
00:15:39,890 --> 00:15:44,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if we don't go into debate over techniques, has that requirement been met?

188
00:15:46,196 --> 00:15:48,618
[SPEAKER_00]: If no, there is no discussion with you then.

189
00:15:48,959 --> 00:15:52,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whatever you talk about meditation, it's not what meditation is.

190
00:15:52,542 --> 00:15:53,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have no base for it.

191
00:15:53,583 --> 00:15:58,068
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically like when the... and the Buddha said to the monk who was asked about

192
00:15:59,347 --> 00:16:01,769
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the pleasure of being withdrawn from sensuality?

193
00:16:01,809 --> 00:16:02,890
[SPEAKER_00]: And this man couldn't quite explain.

194
00:16:02,910 --> 00:16:08,396
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, well, if you fully understood it, the similes that you haven't had before, you would have... they would have occurred to you.

195
00:16:08,996 --> 00:16:11,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, imagine you come with a man to the bottom of the hill.

196
00:16:12,232 --> 00:16:20,136
[SPEAKER_00]: You climb to the top of the hill, he stays at the bottom, and you tell him, oh, dear friend, the view from this top of the hill, it's immeasurable.

197
00:16:20,496 --> 00:16:25,278
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I can see the valleys, and oceans, and forests, it's just heavenly.

198
00:16:25,858 --> 00:16:28,680
[SPEAKER_00]: And the man at the bottom turns around, and all he sees is tree trunks.

199
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:32,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, no, I don't, I don't, I disagree, I don't see that.

200
00:16:33,322 --> 00:16:39,945
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if this man had compassion for the man at the bottom, he would come down the hill, grab him by the arm, drag him up the hill, and see for himself.

201
00:16:40,866 --> 00:16:44,310
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the simile was being withdrawn from sensuality, you see for yourself.

202
00:16:45,291 --> 00:16:47,193
[SPEAKER_00]: But most people are like the man at the bottom.

203
00:16:47,613 --> 00:16:52,438
[SPEAKER_00]: They argue the view from the top, while they're still deep down in the forest of sensuality.

204
00:16:53,901 --> 00:16:56,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that in itself is already a form of meditation.

205
00:16:56,363 --> 00:16:58,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense restraint, throughout the day.

206
00:16:58,585 --> 00:17:05,070
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot possibly do that without having developed proper imperturbable mindfulness.

207
00:17:05,170 --> 00:17:09,554
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is imperturbable mindfulness other than composed mindfulness?

208
00:17:10,395 --> 00:17:14,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Other than samādhi, literally, composure, keeping it together.

209
00:17:14,258 --> 00:17:17,841
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something you can actually get your mind around, so to speak.

210
00:17:18,001 --> 00:17:19,722
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, don't do anything wrong.

211
00:17:21,547 --> 00:17:25,369
[SPEAKER_00]: And always be aware, at the corner of your eye, in the background, in your mind.

212
00:17:26,030 --> 00:17:27,991
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm about to do this, I'm doing this, I'm doing...

213
00:17:28,671 --> 00:17:32,874
[SPEAKER_00]: The environment I'm in, am I heading towards the unwholesome or wholesome?

214
00:17:33,695 --> 00:17:37,757
[SPEAKER_00]: If unwholesome, make the effort, rein yourself in, pull yourself out.

215
00:17:38,317 --> 00:17:41,499
[SPEAKER_00]: So... That's where the work is done.

216
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:45,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I say... Am I doing anything wrong?

217
00:17:46,042 --> 00:17:47,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Or am I going to do anything wrong?

218
00:17:48,223 --> 00:17:50,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any unwholesome intentions here?

219
00:17:50,625 --> 00:17:50,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

220
00:17:52,086 --> 00:17:57,009
[SPEAKER_01]: But that requires me to have a specific way of being aware.

221
00:17:57,029 --> 00:17:57,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

222
00:17:58,010 --> 00:17:59,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Being aware of the context.

223
00:17:59,250 --> 00:17:59,991
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I keep saying.

224
00:18:00,391 --> 00:18:09,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, can you, like, if all you do is concentrate what's in front of you, your intentions will become invisible to you, more than they already are.

225
00:18:09,817 --> 00:18:12,399
[SPEAKER_00]: You can never attend your intention.

226
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:17,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason you're attending things is because you intended to do so.

227
00:18:20,345 --> 00:18:23,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention is always in the background.

228
00:18:23,647 --> 00:18:25,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention is always the state of mind.

229
00:18:26,469 --> 00:18:30,571
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole connection between cetanā and citta, etymologically speaking.

230
00:18:31,151 --> 00:18:32,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention and the mind.

231
00:18:33,554 --> 00:18:36,175
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be aware of your intention.

232
00:18:36,215 --> 00:18:36,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

233
00:18:36,516 --> 00:18:37,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not something you concentrate on.

234
00:18:38,417 --> 00:18:43,239
[SPEAKER_00]: That's only something you can know as it's there and during, as the current context of your experience.

235
00:18:43,559 --> 00:18:57,807
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this type of awareness, of being able to be aware of your intentions behind your actions, it's a kind of basic tool for a

236
00:18:59,522 --> 00:19:04,264
[SPEAKER_01]: A person that's going to be wholesome or responsible person.

237
00:19:04,444 --> 00:19:04,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

238
00:19:05,145 --> 00:19:05,985
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the first step.

239
00:19:07,185 --> 00:19:09,226
[SPEAKER_00]: That's being self-aware perpetually.

240
00:19:12,228 --> 00:19:13,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Being self-aware.

241
00:19:13,688 --> 00:19:22,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is your constant practice of concentration, again in inverted commas, whatever it is, is that improving that self-awareness that you have throughout the day?

242
00:19:22,952 --> 00:19:28,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it making you completely, like, sort of lapse and go in a state of unawareness?

243
00:19:29,493 --> 00:19:41,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when the Buddha described how Sariputta went through all the jhānas, he described that every single one of those jhānas, the most refined ones, were fully understood, fully known, mindfulness was clear as a day throughout it.

244
00:19:41,736 --> 00:19:43,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So that can be another criteria.

245
00:19:43,097 --> 00:19:50,098
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can't quite shake off your doing the ānāpānasati or something, well, make sure your self-awareness is not compromised.

246
00:19:53,319 --> 00:19:55,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowingly he breathes in, knowingly he breathes out.

247
00:19:57,423 --> 00:20:06,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you aware even more of your current situation when you're doing your technique, method of meditation?

248
00:20:06,128 --> 00:20:08,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, basically, the world is shut out?

249
00:20:08,409 --> 00:20:10,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you in a narrow mind?

250
00:20:10,351 --> 00:20:11,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what comes up.

251
00:20:11,711 --> 00:20:12,512
[SPEAKER_00]: I said that before.

252
00:20:12,552 --> 00:20:13,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, one-pointedness.

253
00:20:14,113 --> 00:20:16,454
[SPEAKER_00]: But is it one wide point that includes everything?

254
00:20:17,275 --> 00:20:19,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Or one narrow point that shuts everything out?

255
00:20:21,965 --> 00:20:28,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you... when I read any, like, most of the contemporary instructions, it was always about the direction of shutting everything else out.

256
00:20:28,908 --> 00:20:39,874
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it goes hand in hand with stop thinking, stop thinking, too much thinking, just focus, just focus, just concentrate, repeat the mantra, concentrate, become basically a thoughtless, half-asleep...

257
00:20:39,894 --> 00:20:46,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing this thing, doing this thing, doing this... Just do it, repetitively, until you go into that mental stupor, so to speak.

258
00:20:48,321 --> 00:20:53,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to develop right view, and I start restraining the senses.

259
00:20:53,643 --> 00:20:54,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Seem the danger, the slightest fault.

260
00:20:54,844 --> 00:20:59,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Restraining the senses will already make you wakeful, mindful, and alert.

261
00:20:59,866 --> 00:21:02,107
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already samādhi, to begin with.

262
00:21:03,787 --> 00:21:14,932
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, within that, like, when that's not taking too much of your energy, when the mind is established, when the Brucellian mind is pliable, and not lighter, and not heavy, then you could okay.

263
00:21:16,735 --> 00:21:17,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Read the suttas.

264
00:21:17,495 --> 00:21:20,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Take other things that, in your mind, need to be understood.

265
00:21:21,017 --> 00:21:22,618
[SPEAKER_00]: What is suffering?

266
00:21:22,638 --> 00:21:23,879
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the origin of suffering?

267
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:32,384
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the... what is the basically... any aspect of the suttas they would be talking about?

268
00:21:32,424 --> 00:21:33,144
[SPEAKER_00]: What is anicca?

269
00:21:33,885 --> 00:21:34,545
[SPEAKER_00]: What is dukkha?

270
00:21:34,565 --> 00:21:36,946
[SPEAKER_00]: What is... like, understand it.

271
00:21:37,667 --> 00:21:38,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Make the effort.

272
00:21:38,487 --> 00:21:43,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that properly developed basis of sīla, sense of strength, mindfulness and samādhi.

273
00:21:44,742 --> 00:21:48,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are doing your wisdom practice now.

274
00:21:48,143 --> 00:21:49,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I trying to understand?

275
00:21:50,003 --> 00:21:53,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I watching my nostrils because I'm basically tired of trying to understand?

276
00:21:55,964 --> 00:21:58,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And yes, sometimes you might be tired of trying to understand.

277
00:21:58,985 --> 00:21:59,385
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

278
00:22:00,105 --> 00:22:01,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be taking too much effort.

279
00:22:01,485 --> 00:22:02,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, okay, stop it.

280
00:22:02,805 --> 00:22:08,526
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you stop your sensory restraint, control of your senses and daily mindfulness.

281
00:22:08,546 --> 00:22:09,607
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't stop your samādhi.

282
00:22:09,627 --> 00:22:10,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't stop your self-awareness.

283
00:22:13,159 --> 00:22:22,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you don't have to actively, if you can, if you cannot, but it doesn't mean, oh, but now I am justified in going into the state of oblivion and forgetting about myself.

284
00:22:22,972 --> 00:22:26,697
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like what we spoke in that other talk, I forgot the title, like, you never rest.

285
00:22:28,377 --> 00:22:29,438
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean you always work.

286
00:22:29,698 --> 00:22:31,539
[SPEAKER_00]: It just simply means you refuse, basically.

287
00:22:31,999 --> 00:22:37,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when you don't work, you refuse to give in to that distraction and self-delusion where you forget about yourself.

288
00:22:37,842 --> 00:22:39,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you're always diligent.

289
00:22:39,804 --> 00:22:40,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you never rest.

290
00:22:41,164 --> 00:22:43,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, that type of, basically, effort is effortless.

291
00:22:44,246 --> 00:22:45,287
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't tire you out.

292
00:22:46,187 --> 00:22:47,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction tires you out.

293
00:22:49,352 --> 00:22:50,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality ties you out.

294
00:22:51,533 --> 00:22:53,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Misguided effort ties you out.

295
00:22:53,654 --> 00:22:56,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness, done rightly, it's effortless and you can't have too much of it.

296
00:22:56,875 --> 00:22:57,955
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the way I would say.

297
00:22:58,475 --> 00:23:03,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, another thing is, you could re-evaluate when you... when there are certain practices that you find helpful.

298
00:23:05,378 --> 00:23:09,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is helpful to me?

299
00:23:10,279 --> 00:23:12,480
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the helpfulness aspect of those practices?

300
00:23:13,240 --> 00:23:15,761
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my measure for helpfulness?

301
00:23:15,881 --> 00:23:16,801
[SPEAKER_00]: What's my criteria?

302
00:23:17,602 --> 00:23:18,222
[SPEAKER_00]: What's my value?

303
00:23:20,533 --> 00:23:21,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it how it makes me feel?

304
00:23:22,094 --> 00:23:29,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do I see it like genuinely supporting my sense restraint, composure, clarity of mindfulness in a day-to-day life?

305
00:23:31,417 --> 00:23:32,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I don't quite see it.

306
00:23:33,477 --> 00:23:38,339
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't make the effort to see it in itself, it cannot present itself to you.

307
00:23:38,399 --> 00:23:41,180
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to do it.

308
00:23:46,841 --> 00:23:48,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So how does this

309
00:23:50,804 --> 00:24:00,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the danger in a slightest fault, sense, restraint, relate to yoniso manisikāra.

310
00:24:02,393 --> 00:24:11,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's that, where I said that, that first step, having the knowledge of the context and not being absorbed in the object of your concentration or attention.

311
00:24:13,382 --> 00:24:14,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing where your intentions are.

312
00:24:16,954 --> 00:24:18,897
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the beginning of yoniso.

313
00:24:18,977 --> 00:24:25,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing where your intentions are enduring, because they are there, enduring.

314
00:24:27,109 --> 00:24:30,895
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowing the background, discerning the peripheral.

315
00:24:36,802 --> 00:24:37,923
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much it.

316
00:24:38,444 --> 00:24:41,307
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, if you practice that, you will get to see the sign of your mind.

317
00:24:41,768 --> 00:24:49,817
[SPEAKER_00]: The more clear seeing of your intentions you are, cetanā, the clearer citta will become outlined.

318
00:24:51,501 --> 00:24:55,143
[SPEAKER_01]: And the clearer the setup is... What setup?

319
00:24:55,903 --> 00:24:59,725
[SPEAKER_01]: The framework of experience, the... Well, that's what the mind is.

320
00:25:00,025 --> 00:25:00,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

321
00:25:00,505 --> 00:25:05,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, the setup or the way things are situated.

322
00:25:06,108 --> 00:25:06,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

323
00:25:06,488 --> 00:25:09,030
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's determined basically pretty much by the state of the mind.

324
00:25:09,050 --> 00:25:10,650
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the mind is the forerunner.

325
00:25:11,511 --> 00:25:15,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the prerequisite for the right view is that grasping the sign of your mind.

326
00:25:17,657 --> 00:25:27,687
[SPEAKER_00]: The outlines of your mind, seeing it for what it is, enduring there, not abstractly, not as a speculative thought, not as a mystical experience at the end of your concentration effort.

327
00:25:28,147 --> 00:25:31,331
[SPEAKER_00]: No, right there and then, my mind is such, and you see it.

328
00:25:31,551 --> 00:25:34,754
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the suchness of it.

329
00:25:36,556 --> 00:25:37,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't interfere with it.

330
00:25:38,478 --> 00:25:40,660
[SPEAKER_00]: The state of mind is first.

331
00:25:42,497 --> 00:25:44,178
[SPEAKER_00]: That you, by the way, did not create.

332
00:25:44,679 --> 00:25:45,479
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subjected to it.

333
00:25:45,519 --> 00:25:47,320
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, we're subjected to this weather.

334
00:25:47,841 --> 00:25:48,862
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes and goes.

335
00:25:50,323 --> 00:25:51,483
[SPEAKER_00]: It arises, it ceases.

336
00:25:51,543 --> 00:25:52,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It increases, it decreases.

337
00:25:52,604 --> 00:25:53,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

338
00:25:54,325 --> 00:26:07,635
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, for somebody who doesn't see sign of their mind, doesn't see the mind in suchness as enduring, elated mind is elated mind, depressed mind is depressed mind, happy mind is happy mind, satipaṭṭhāna, so to say, they put themselves first.

339
00:26:08,155 --> 00:26:09,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's, I own this mind.

340
00:26:11,037 --> 00:26:11,978
[SPEAKER_00]: This mind is mine.

341
00:26:12,939 --> 00:26:13,780
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you suffer.

342
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:18,322
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind that you don't want, basically, is directing in the direction that it's unpleasant.

343
00:26:20,224 --> 00:26:27,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're being influenced and pressured and affected by the unpleasant state of mind.

344
00:26:28,049 --> 00:26:34,132
[SPEAKER_00]: By not as lofty as you want it to be, not as light as you want it to be, not as motivated as you want it to be.

345
00:26:35,113 --> 00:26:38,035
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to just see mind for what it is, you would not be affected by it.

346
00:26:40,030 --> 00:26:44,212
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only because you keep implicitly, sort of, entertaining the notion of ownership over it.

347
00:26:44,492 --> 00:26:50,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you are familiar with that order... Well, that's... Yeah.

348
00:26:50,555 --> 00:26:52,916
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to cultivate it.

349
00:26:52,936 --> 00:26:54,877
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like one and done deal.

350
00:26:55,077 --> 00:26:56,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you see it once and it's done, finished.

351
00:26:57,438 --> 00:27:06,883
[SPEAKER_00]: You see it once and you have to then keep restoring the order because your habits, your asavas will keep, sort of, washing your way away from that order, into the wrong order, so to speak.

352
00:27:08,542 --> 00:27:19,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Same like going against the stream, you have to sort of persist in an even application of effort to go against the stream until you reach the end of that stream.

353
00:27:21,327 --> 00:27:24,569
[SPEAKER_00]: If your effort lapses, the stream will pick you up and drag you down again.

354
00:27:25,429 --> 00:27:26,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, same with the right order.

355
00:27:26,570 --> 00:27:27,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to sustain it.

356
00:27:29,591 --> 00:27:36,474
[SPEAKER_00]: But seeing the right order on the basis of seeing your mind in its suchness is the beginning towards the right view.

357
00:27:41,519 --> 00:27:43,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that's what the right view is.

358
00:27:43,460 --> 00:27:44,501
[SPEAKER_00]: The right seeing, the right order.

359
00:27:45,501 --> 00:27:47,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing what comes first.

360
00:27:47,462 --> 00:27:50,904
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why those people who got the right view after Buddha would speak.

361
00:27:50,924 --> 00:27:51,765
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, it's amazing.

362
00:27:52,425 --> 00:27:57,748
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like Master Gautama putting first that which was second, and putting second that which was first.

363
00:27:57,788 --> 00:28:00,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting that which was upside down, putting down, and that which was down, putting up.

364
00:28:01,490 --> 00:28:01,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah!

365
00:28:02,891 --> 00:28:03,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Fixing it.

366
00:28:08,150 --> 00:28:11,391
[SPEAKER_01]: You're turning upright.

367
00:28:15,813 --> 00:28:17,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you would know now what to do.

368
00:28:18,814 --> 00:28:23,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Sustain, protect and develop that order, which is the Noble Eightfold Path.

369
00:28:26,036 --> 00:28:31,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't cause the disorder through lust, aversion, distraction, through intentions based on lust, aversion and distraction.

370
00:28:33,198 --> 00:28:36,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You abstain from it and disorder will become solidified.

371
00:28:36,179 --> 00:28:38,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where does, then, ānāpānasati come in?

372
00:28:39,440 --> 00:28:46,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, ānāpānasati comes in for somebody who understood the right order and they want to develop the further calmness of the mind.

373
00:28:47,442 --> 00:28:52,084
[SPEAKER_00]: The further, like, non-perturbability of their mindfulness.

374
00:28:53,304 --> 00:28:57,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what ānāpānasati fortifies.

375
00:28:58,846 --> 00:28:59,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Your satipaṭṭhānas.

376
00:29:02,906 --> 00:29:04,647
[SPEAKER_00]: They have brought a fulfillment through your...

377
00:29:05,007 --> 00:29:05,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of...

378
00:29:05,987 --> 00:29:12,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically just doing the same practice of mindfulness of satipaṭṭhāna, just doing it on the level of your breath.

379
00:29:12,610 --> 00:29:12,950
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

380
00:29:13,010 --> 00:29:14,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what ānāpānasati is.

381
00:29:14,231 --> 00:29:19,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same practice, just instead of going up and down and about, you just sit still and you do it within this.

382
00:29:20,173 --> 00:29:21,153
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same practice.

383
00:29:21,173 --> 00:29:21,793
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said.

384
00:29:22,614 --> 00:29:28,816
[SPEAKER_00]: When, like, that body that's there, when you are mindful of your breath, is body that stands for the body.

385
00:29:28,856 --> 00:29:30,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Body among bodies, how it's translated.

386
00:29:31,645 --> 00:29:37,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And you you're mindful of that body correctly, means you're seeing the characteristics of that body correctly.

387
00:29:37,246 --> 00:29:38,266
[SPEAKER_00]: The suchness of it.

388
00:29:39,147 --> 00:29:41,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Without your ownership, without your interference.

389
00:29:41,827 --> 00:29:47,148
[SPEAKER_00]: You stay with that sight long enough, any notion of ownership and interference will fade away.

390
00:29:47,248 --> 00:29:49,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion of lust will fade away.

391
00:29:49,129 --> 00:29:52,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion of appropriation will fade away.

392
00:29:52,249 --> 00:29:55,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's... that satipaṭṭhāna, ānāpānasati, is based upon

393
00:29:57,298 --> 00:30:02,142
[SPEAKER_01]: This prerequisite should be done on this prerequisite of seeing the mind.

394
00:30:02,763 --> 00:30:10,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, regardless of which concentration you do, it's all about fortifying your mindfulness, you know, the mindfulness that you supposedly have before your concentration.

395
00:30:11,930 --> 00:30:19,537
[SPEAKER_00]: If you read a suttas, you'll see basically that mindfulness is that which gets developed to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

396
00:30:21,126 --> 00:30:38,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Either through sharp wisdom, either through sense restraint, determination and so on, samādhi, any form of samādhi, it's basically, done rightly, it's the samādhi that fortifies your mindfulness, the clarity of your recollection and your awareness, self-awareness, yeah.

397
00:30:40,138 --> 00:30:48,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ask yourself, with your current practice of meditation, is your concentration fortifying your day-to-day mindfulness, or is it completely opposite direction of it?

398
00:30:48,828 --> 00:30:55,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you emerge from your concentration, and now you may start making the effort to be mindful, and be present, and be aware of your intentions.

399
00:30:56,797 --> 00:30:59,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what your samādhi should be fortifying, not something else.

400
00:31:00,159 --> 00:31:03,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's vipassana, whether it's samādhi, either way, basically, you're developing.

401
00:31:03,982 --> 00:31:04,742
[SPEAKER_00]: One brings the other.

402
00:31:04,802 --> 00:31:11,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're making effort actively to understand, or just fortifying your mindfulness without active contemplation, you're developing the same understanding.

403
00:31:13,488 --> 00:31:19,472
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, with introduction of more and more commentaries and interpretations, and obviously two and a half millennia,

404
00:31:20,374 --> 00:31:29,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, it's like this whole view of, oh, this is my study, this is my formal practice, my mantra, my this, and you have no idea how any of that relates.

405
00:31:29,483 --> 00:31:36,310
[SPEAKER_00]: You just hope that all these things that make you feel good, make you feel Buddhist and a committed practitioner, will somehow result in your enlightenment.

406
00:31:37,260 --> 00:31:53,880
[SPEAKER_00]: It's no different than to, like, you know, when martial arts back in the day existed, originated, in the form of self-defense, or during the wartime, but then, couple of millennia, they got so mystified, and now people have these hollow forms, doing these hollow forms, unable, like, to even defend themselves.

407
00:31:54,080 --> 00:31:55,522
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no practical value of it.

408
00:31:56,477 --> 00:31:56,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

409
00:31:57,017 --> 00:32:07,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, formal meditation today is completely the opposite of your self-defense that you would apply in a day, which is sense restraint, and your mindfulness, and your awareness of your intentions.

410
00:32:08,382 --> 00:32:09,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you do it then?

411
00:32:09,923 --> 00:32:22,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't you just find a self-defense class that will teach you self-defense, as opposed to, like, venerating some two years old, two millennia old tradition that's divorced from practicality, and then go and have to do the actual self-defense.

412
00:32:22,788 --> 00:32:23,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

413
00:32:23,568 --> 00:32:25,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, do your samādhi as much as you want.

414
00:32:26,245 --> 00:32:32,528
[SPEAKER_00]: But, do it in a sense that you can sustain it throughout your day, even when you're not sitting in your protected safe environment.

415
00:32:33,169 --> 00:32:34,729
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the samādhi you should cultivate.

416
00:32:35,170 --> 00:32:38,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the samādhi that's at least closer to make you imperturbable.

417
00:32:39,132 --> 00:32:41,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually, your mindfulness imperturbable.

418
00:32:42,013 --> 00:32:51,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, now I am justified in, in, in, like basically shutting everything else out and engaging in my practice of concentration to calm myself down.

419
00:32:52,078 --> 00:32:54,240
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when I re-emerge from my formal practice,

420
00:32:55,205 --> 00:33:05,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I have to restart my mindfulness, my sense restraint, and remind myself of the values I need to maintain throughout the day as a form of self-defense.

421
00:33:05,276 --> 00:33:07,539
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the samādhi that you should be cultivating.

422
00:33:07,659 --> 00:33:08,280
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

423
00:33:09,917 --> 00:33:15,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Go straight, cultivate that which will basically develop further, that which you want to develop.

424
00:33:15,981 --> 00:33:22,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do a meditation, as I said, that you can then spread throughout your day, even when you're not formally sitting.

425
00:33:23,387 --> 00:33:26,629
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, there should be no difference between formal and informal meditation.

426
00:33:26,649 --> 00:33:27,390
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing.

427
00:33:27,690 --> 00:33:28,851
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing.

428
00:33:29,031 --> 00:33:34,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Your body is just doing different... And the Buddha says so many times, so many times, like, he establishes his mind in jhāna,

429
00:33:36,006 --> 00:33:39,807
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the perfect context, withdrawn from sensuality, imperturbable.

430
00:33:40,367 --> 00:33:48,449
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whether he sits, eats, goes to toilet, extends his arm, whatever he does is divine, because the state of his mind is divine.

431
00:33:49,369 --> 00:33:51,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Second jhāna, third jhāna, same.

432
00:33:52,709 --> 00:33:53,170
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there you go.

433
00:33:53,350 --> 00:33:56,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Establish your money jhāna, and then go about, and see how long your jhāna will last.

434
00:34:01,671 --> 00:34:04,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that... Again, if you don't take anything I say,

435
00:34:06,215 --> 00:34:10,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this should at least... you could reason that this is the right thing to do.

436
00:34:10,458 --> 00:34:31,932
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you disagree with everything else, the advice that your meditation should basically, theoretically, be sustainable throughout your day, throughout your time, and should certainly be... should be improving your mindfulness and everything else, that is a valid... like, you cannot find fault with that.

437
00:34:33,933 --> 00:34:39,698
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't take anything else I say, well, at least use this and revise your meditation practice.

438
00:34:41,380 --> 00:34:44,282
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, it doesn't matter, oh, I must find out this teacher's wrong.

439
00:34:44,703 --> 00:34:45,484
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all irrelevant.

440
00:34:45,744 --> 00:34:47,906
[SPEAKER_00]: What's relevant is why you are doing it for yourself.

441
00:34:49,627 --> 00:34:52,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you aware of the motives behind and how you're doing it?

442
00:34:52,310 --> 00:34:55,773
[SPEAKER_00]: What's your, as I said, implication of the method behind?

443
00:34:57,552 --> 00:35:04,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you hoping it will magically result, or protect you, or whatever else, by some sort of lofty development as a product of it, or...

444
00:35:04,974 --> 00:35:05,874
[SPEAKER_00]: But do you see it?

445
00:35:07,715 --> 00:35:08,115
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

446
00:35:08,295 --> 00:35:10,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you losing your self-awareness when you're practicing it?

447
00:35:11,216 --> 00:35:15,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you increasing your self-awareness, even when you stop practicing it, on account of your previous practice?

448
00:35:16,457 --> 00:35:22,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Are your intentions clearer to spot, as a result of your samādhi practice beforehand, or you have to redo the same effort every time?

449
00:35:24,119 --> 00:35:26,540
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't find fault with that, because that's exactly what everybody was saying.

450
00:35:27,755 --> 00:35:32,943
[SPEAKER_00]: You should always know what you're doing, why you're doing, and abstain from doing unwholesome and cultivate wholesome.

451
00:35:33,244 --> 00:35:35,207
[SPEAKER_00]: You should never lose that context at the back of your mind.

452
00:35:36,329 --> 00:35:40,836
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no amount of samādhi and concentration is justifiable to take you away from that.

453
00:35:42,943 --> 00:35:44,483
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, oh, yeah, quite the opposite.

454
00:35:44,844 --> 00:35:48,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Samādhi and concentration should be bringing you even closer to knowing those things.

455
00:35:49,665 --> 00:35:55,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is your samādhi bringing you closer to knowing the states of your mind, knowing your intention behind actions, when you're not sitting and meditating?

456
00:35:56,006 --> 00:35:56,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Continuously.

457
00:35:57,407 --> 00:36:00,068
[SPEAKER_00]: How continuous that practice is?

458
00:36:00,388 --> 00:36:01,528
[SPEAKER_00]: How continuously can you do it?

459
00:36:02,608 --> 00:36:04,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you can only do it when you're in a protected environment.

460
00:36:08,367 --> 00:36:21,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are overdoing your concentration with this new value of finding the common ground that you can sustain, whether formal or informal, it will basically pull you out of your overdoing extent, so to speak.

461
00:36:21,183 --> 00:36:22,425
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were overdoing it before,

462
00:36:23,402 --> 00:36:31,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, when you realize, oh, but I should be able to sustain it throughout the day, you will step back from that overly concentrated self, like absorbed, where you lose the sight of your situation.

463
00:36:31,989 --> 00:36:39,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you pull back into that, okay, so that's to the extent I can practice these calming techniques and so on, without over committing.

464
00:36:40,476 --> 00:36:46,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, even when I'm not and end up sitting and meditating, I can still sustain the same self-awareness.

465
00:36:47,763 --> 00:36:50,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's composure, literally, keeping it together.

466
00:36:51,317 --> 00:37:01,840
[SPEAKER_00]: But you wouldn't say to somebody, oh, you can keep things together, when you shut everybody out, lock yourself inside, spend three hours preparing, and then, no, means you're quite precious.

467
00:37:01,860 --> 00:37:08,602
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who, amidst of things falling apart, keeps it together, is the one who can keep it together, is the one who has samādhi.

468
00:37:10,683 --> 00:37:11,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in colloquial terms.

469
00:37:15,425 --> 00:37:17,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, keep it together, man, when people say that.

470
00:37:22,154 --> 00:37:25,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I can't keep it together as long as I'm shut in my room.

471
00:37:25,715 --> 00:37:27,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, like, it's only a relative.

472
00:37:27,235 --> 00:37:28,795
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not really keeping it together.

473
00:37:29,456 --> 00:37:31,816
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to keep it together regardless of the circumstances.

474
00:37:32,256 --> 00:37:34,317
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the imperturbable samādhi.

475
00:37:36,257 --> 00:37:37,337
[SPEAKER_00]: The unshakable mindfulness.

476
00:37:39,918 --> 00:37:40,298
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

477
00:37:40,798 --> 00:37:48,620
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that single-handedly will make you stop overthinking about methods and techniques, because it's irrelevant, actually.

478
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:49,580
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize.

479
00:37:50,397 --> 00:37:52,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I watch my nostrils for four seconds or eight seconds?

480
00:37:52,759 --> 00:38:05,593
[SPEAKER_00]: That only matters to somebody who hopes that this magical product, a result of their concentration, mystical result, basically, they will be equated with knowledge and wisdom and liberation.

481
00:38:05,793 --> 00:38:05,953
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

482
00:38:11,477 --> 00:38:13,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize most of it doesn't matter.

483
00:38:13,339 --> 00:38:19,363
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is you making the effort to sustain your mindfulness in controlled environment and less controlled environment.

484
00:38:19,844 --> 00:38:21,965
[SPEAKER_00]: In formal environment or informal environment.

485
00:38:22,286 --> 00:38:23,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Same mindfulness.

486
00:38:24,107 --> 00:38:26,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the same mind, by the way.

487
00:38:27,290 --> 00:38:29,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Same awareness, full alert.

488
00:38:29,331 --> 00:38:30,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

489
00:38:31,733 --> 00:38:37,398
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can develop, if you can only sustain it in a protected environment,

490
00:38:38,233 --> 00:38:39,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, but you recognize it.

491
00:38:39,354 --> 00:38:48,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so it's quite dependent on certain circumstances, which means if those circumstances change, my mindfulness will go, and I better make further effort to approve that dependence.

492
00:38:49,543 --> 00:38:54,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, if I'm not able to meet my requirements, I will fall apart.

493
00:38:55,708 --> 00:39:00,232
[SPEAKER_00]: And then my sense restraint will suffer, my sīla will suffer, and everything else will suffer.

